Subject: Spatiotemporal model running slowly with random walk per district
Message-ID: <VuRYlgNSWM4.t1IpAz1aAQAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/VuRYlgNSWM4/m/t1IpAz1aAQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Dear all,

I have been fitting the following Poisson model with a single age group with about 80 million deaths. It works, but it takes around 72 hours. I have 20 age groups to fit, so it would take around 2 months to fit fully. Are there any tricks that I am missing to make the following run more quickly?

Best,

Robbie

fml <- deaths ~

# global terms

        1 +                                                                                                                                          # global intercept

        time +                                                                                                                                     # global slope

# month specific terms

        f(month, model="rw1",cyclic = TRUE,control.group=list(model='besag')) +                           # month specific intercept (spatially-corellated)

        f(month2, time2, model="rw1",cyclic = TRUE,control.group=list(model='besag')) +              # month specific slope (spatially-corellated)

# state specific terms

        f(district, model="bym",graph=USA.adj) +                                                                              # district specific intercept (BYM)

        f(district2, time2, model="bym",graph=USA.adj) +                                                                  # district specific slope (BYM)

# random walks terms

        f(time3, model="rw1") +                                                                                                         # national rw1

        f(time4, model="rw1", group=district3, control.group=list(model="I")) +                                 # rw1 per district

# overdispersion term

        f(e, model = "iid")                                                                                                                   # overdispersion term

mod3 <-

    inla(formula = fml,

    family = "poisson",

    data = data,

    E = population,

    control.compute = list(dic=TRUE),

    control.predictor = list(link = 1),

    verbose=TRUE

    )

